Three implications in your proposed justification fail.

First, a marginal bound does not multiply across a target list. Two types of fields may descend complementary types of primes, while every split contains both types. Each marginal success probability can be \(1/2\), yet no complete split descends.

Second, independence of the **raw** split from \((m,f)\) does not imply independence after acceptance conditions involving that field. Nor does it make the primes within one split independent.

Third, the target-prime law is not automatically proportional to \(1/\ell\). Even for ordinary integers conditioned to be smooth, saddle-point weights involve approximately \(\ell^{-\sigma}\), together with a product constraint. Your shortest-vector distribution introduces another conditioning. Polynomial discrepancies per prime can accumulate at the main \(L[2/5]\) scale.

Your stated values of \(k\) are reasonable heuristic orders, with logarithmic factors suppressed. They are not deterministic bounds.

**The proposed LV first-moment transfer has a parameter-range obstruction.**

For your fixed-degree regime, put \(t=a-bm\). The randomized polynomial satisfies
\[
F(a,b)=\widehat F(a,b)+tP(a,b),
\]
and, for primitive pairs,
\[
F(a,b)\equiv nb^d\pmod t.
\]
Thus randomizing coefficients leaves the value in a prescribed progression modulo **\(t=\ell c\)**, not merely modulo the smooth cofactor \(c\).

