**(a) Uniformity and hidden losses.** Write \(T=\sqrt{\log N\log\log N}\). For fixed \(d,k\), the factors
\[
2^\omega,\quad(\log N)^{O(k)},\quad c_k,\quad\gamma^{-4k}
\]
are indeed \(e^{o(T)}\), uniformly if the hypothesis on \(\gamma\) is uniform. For growing \(k\), the actual requirements include
\[
k\log\log N=o(T),\qquad k\log(1/\gamma)=o(T).
\]
Merely writing \(\gamma=L^{-o(1)}\) does not establish the second condition.

The irregular-lattice estimate also needs its finite-interval error: \(m\) is sampled from an interval, not uniformly modulo \(\ell\). The FM regime supplies polynomial room \(M\gg\ell\), so this is repairable. Excluding irregular lattices must be incorporated into the moment estimates, rather than justified solely by an unconditional probability bound.

The field-selection algorithm is insufficiently specified. Starting with \(\tau=L^{-1}\) and halving its exponent can spend a main-scale factor before finding the useful rate. A clean repair avoids estimating that rate: sample fresh fields until one capped attempt succeeds, then retain that field. If \(q_A\) is its success probability and \(\mu=E q_A\), the retained field has law proportional to \(q_A\), and
\[
E[1/q_A\mid\text{retained}]
=\Pr(q_A>0)/\mu\le1/\mu.
\]
Thus \(C\) further successes have the required expected cost when \(\mu=L^{-o(1)}\).

Finally, LP supplies an available **upper bound** for shape testing. No lower bound proves the notes’ stronger wording that every rigorous shape test must pay that cost.

**(b) Does \(D_k\) imply complete descents? Yes, after the above repairs.**

For at least a \(\gamma/2\) proportion of fields, \(g_A\ge\gamma/2\). In each such field, S′ gives
\[
q_A\ge \gamma^{2k}L^{-o(1)}.
\]
Every prime remaining after cancellation belongs to that field’s \(G_A\), so all searches succeed in the same field.

This genuinely answers lemmaM_2’s complementary-field-types counterexample. The construction counts splits entirely within each field’s own good set. It never multiplies marginal probabilities for the original unrestricted split law.

The implication requires \(G_A\) to mean **certified, capped descent success in an admissible field**. A first moment counting smooth norm values alone does not supply that certification. With the common column set and postprocessing assumptions made explicit, DD then applies: all acceptance and dependency choices depend on exposed squares and certificates, preserving hidden-root randomness.

**(c) FM: plausible transplantation, not an established transfer.**

[LV Lemma 8.5](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1805.08873) is stated for smooth moduli. Its maximum over reduced residue classes accommodates moving residues; its modulus restriction does not accommodate \(\ell s\) directly.

The sketch identifies a useful fact: a conductor containing \(\ell>B\) is polynomially large for fixed \(k\), so it can enter the large-conductor range. But several steps remain:

- The smooth variable has length \(Q/\ell\), not \(Q\). The restricted character weights involve smooth densities at \(Q/(\ell r)\). They are not literally LV’s sums multiplied by a common \(\sum_\ell1/\ell\).
- The induced multiplicities of \(t=a-bm\), after primitivity, box restrictions and rational smoothness, need explicit bounds. Counting bad moduli without these weights is insufficient.
- Coprimality, signs, changing value ranges and boundary errors need uniform treatment.

The actual large-conductor input contains
\[
\sqrt{\Psi(x,B)}
\bigl(Q+x^{1/2-\eta}\log^2x\bigr)\log^{7/2}x,
\]
not just the \(Q\sqrt\Psi\) term quoted in the sketch. Fixed polynomial slack can absorb it, but the comparison must be made against the restricted family’s main term. See [Harper, Proposition 2](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1208.5992).

The published [de la Bretèche–Drappeau theorem](https://drappeau.perso.math.cnrs.fr/documents/friable-consec-jems-prefinal-fix1.pdf) cannot simply be cited: setting \(q_0=\ell\), \(y_2=B\) violates \(P^+(q_0)\le y_2\), and its \(e^{O(u_2)}(\log x)^{-A}\) error provides no relative saving here. Raising \(y_2\) to include \(\ell\) enlarges the modulus family and loses the sparse-family information.

I would retain “sketch”, but withdraw “no new idea expected” until the weighted argument is written.

**(d) PC: the identity is right; the proposed algebraic reduction needs more work.**

With \(s=(a-bm)/\ell\),
\[
b's-bs'
=\frac{b'(a-bm)-b(a'-b'm)}{\ell}
=\frac{\Delta}{\ell}.
\]
The sign is correct. The rational problem is therefore a binary smooth-values problem along a line.

The rational/algebraic decomposition is useful bookkeeping, but their averaged bounds cannot simply be multiplied. The algebraic estimate must hold under the weights and conditioning created by rational smoothness, with varying marginal probabilities accounted for.

There is an additional obstacle to the proposed two-dimensional kernel argument. For primitive point pairs,
\[
[\mathbb Z^2:\Gamma]=|\Delta|.
\]
One proof sends the first primitive point to \((1,0)\) by an integral unimodular change of variables and takes the gcd of the transformed minors.

Already for \(d=3\), the primitive coefficient-kernel vector is
\[
